        ["What does current evidence suggest about turning schedules for patients at risk of pressure injury?", ["Frequency should be individualized to risk and skin response", "Turning every 6 hours is adequate for all patients", "Turning is unnecessary on any specialty mattress", "Every patient needs turning exactly every 30 minutes"], "Every 2 hours is a traditional baseline, but guidelines recommend individualized schedules based on risk, surface, and skin checks."],
        ["Which positioning approach is MOST appropriate for a patient with left hemiplegia lying on the affected side?", ["Protract the left scapula so the shoulder is not compressed", "Tuck the left arm under the trunk for support", "Place the left arm behind the back in extension", "Keep the left arm pulled overhead in full flexion"], "Side-lying on the affected side adds sensory input if the shoulder is protracted and free of compression."],
        ["A patient on day 2 after posterior THA is side-lying on the non-surgical side. What is the MOST important item to position?", ["A pillow or wedge between the knees", "A pillow under the surgical knee only", "A small towel roll under the neck", "A firm pillow under the lumbar spine"], "Without a pillow between the legs, the top (surgical) hip falls into adduction and internal rotation, the dislocation position for a posterior approach."],
        ["Which tool is used to predict pressure injury risk?", ["Braden Scale", "Berg Balance Scale", "Modified Ashworth Scale", "Glasgow Coma Scale"], "The Braden Scale scores sensory perception, moisture, activity, mobility, nutrition, and friction/shear. The others measure balance, tone, and consciousness."],
        ["A patient sits at the edge of the bed for the first time after 3 days of bed rest. What should the PT do NEXT before standing?", ["Pause and ask about dizziness while monitoring vitals", "Stand immediately while the patient is motivated", "Have the patient lie back down to rest first", "Apply ankle weights to test lower limb strength"], "Prolonged bed rest raises orthostatic hypotension risk, so check symptoms and vitals before progressing."],
        ["A patient after a transtibial amputation likes to rest with the knee bent over a pillow. What should the PT recommend?", ["Keep the knee extended to prevent flexion contracture", "Continue the position since it is comfortable", "Add a second pillow to increase knee flexion", "Rest in sitting with the knee flexed all day"], "Prolonged knee flexion leads to contracture, which makes prosthetic fitting and gait harder."]
